1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,947
That famous black hole image?

2
00:00:01,947 --> 00:00:04,735
The dark circle is not the black hole itself.

3
00:00:04,915 --> 00:00:05,876
It is a shadow,

4
00:00:05,876 --> 00:00:10,679
created by gravity bending the paths of light around an invisible boundary.

5
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:13,731
In 2019, scientists revealed the first image:

6
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:19,667
a monster at the heart of galaxy M eighty seven, roughly fifty five million light years away.

7
00:00:19,847 --> 00:00:22,626
Its mass is about six and a half billion Suns.

8
00:00:22,626 --> 00:00:27,278
The bright ring comes from hot gas around it, not from the black hole itself.

9
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:31,159
To see it, astronomers linked radio observatories across Earth,

10
00:00:31,159 --> 00:00:34,626
creating a virtual telescope nearly the size of our planet.

11
00:00:34,806 --> 00:00:37,417
This is not a regular camera photograph.

12
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:42,118
Computers reconstructed the image from radio observations taken in 2017.

13
00:00:42,298 --> 00:00:44,957
Beyond the event horizon, light cannot escape.

14
00:00:44,957 --> 00:00:48,542
But the dark region in the image is larger than that boundary.

15
00:00:48,722 --> 00:00:53,118
We cannot see the black hole directly. We see what it does to light.

16
00:00:53,118 --> 00:00:56,092
Save this and subscribe for more real science.
